// ============================================================================
== 2.1 The Eight Types, Named

Every comprehension question on the paper is one of eight. The type is not trivia: it tells you which sentence to read and which options are built to catch you. A candidate who names the type before reading the options answers in twenty seconds; a candidate who does not answers in ninety and often answers wrongly.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch02_types.svg",
  "The eight question types, what each one asks for, and where the answer sits. Reading this table downwards is a reading plan for any passage.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("Name it, then answer it")[
  + Read the question and decide the type before you look at the options.
  + *Detail, vocabulary and reference* are the three types answered by finding a sentence. Scan, read the sentence twice, then choose.
  + *Inference, tone and purpose* are answered by reading a sentence and taking one step from it — never two.
  + *Negation* is a detail question turned inside out: three options are in the passage and one is not.
  + *Main idea* is answered from the whole passage, and its wrong options are details.
]

#concept-box("Why the type comes before the answer")[
  A question's type tells you where its answer lives and what shape it has. A detail answer is a fact: one sentence, no judgement. A tone answer is an adjective: three or four words, no sentence. A purpose answer is a verb: *to warn*, *to correct*, *to compare*. A main-idea answer is a claim covering every paragraph. If you know which of those four shapes you are looking for, three of the four options can be discarded on shape alone — before you have read a word of the passage for the second time.
]

// ============================================================================
== 2.2 The Three Questions Answered by Finding a Sentence

Detail, vocabulary and reference look different and are solved the same way: find the sentence, read it twice, and put the option back into it.

#shortcut-box("Finding the sentence in ten seconds")[
  + *Detail:* scan for the question's key word — a name, a date, a figure, a quoted phrase. It will appear once.
  + *Vocabulary:* find the line, then read the whole clause. The word's sense is decided by the words around it, never by the dictionary.
  + *Reference:* find the pronoun, then look back one or two sentences for a noun that agrees in number and makes sense when substituted.
]

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch02_reference.svg",
  "Four pronouns and where each one points. A reference question is a substitution test, and the sentence itself tells you whether the substitution is right.", width: 100%)

#trap-box("The word with two lives")[
  Words such as *sanction*, *cleave*, *table*, *fine*, *present* and *sound* have two unrelated senses, and the examiner chooses them on purpose. *Sanction* means approve when it is a verb of permission and penalise when it is a verb or noun of punishment; *table* means to propose in British usage and to postpone in American. The passage always gives you the clue — *refused to sanction the levy until the accounts are published* — and a candidate who answers from memory answers the other life of the word.
]

// ============================================================================
== 2.3 Negation: the Question That Is Turned Inside Out

Four words turn a question round: EXCEPT, NOT, FALSE and LEAST. A negation question gives you three statements that are in the passage and one that is not, and asks for the odd one out. It is a detail question with the polarity reversed, and it is the most-missed question on the paper because nothing about it looks different.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch02_negation.svg",
  "The four negative words, and what each one asks for. Mark the word before you read a single option.", width: 100%)

#rule-box("Working a negation question")[
  + Underline or note the negative word as soon as you see it.
  + Read the four options and mark each *in* or *out* against the passage — one word in the margin is enough.
  + Three will be *in*; the *out* is the answer.
  + If you find two *out*, you have misread the passage: go back to the sentence, not to your memory.
  + Never answer a negation question from a first impression. It is the one type that rewards the second look.
]

// ============================================================================
== 2.4 The Four Shapes of Wrong Option

Three of the four options on any comprehension question are built from four templates. Naming the template is usually faster than re-reading the passage, because each template has a tell.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch02_option_shapes.svg",
  "The four shapes of wrong option, how each one is built, and the check that sees through it.", width: 100%)

#worked-example(
  1,
  "The detail, and the sentence it lives in",
  [Read the passage, then answer the question.

    #v(3pt)
    #block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-light, stroke: 0.6pt + jamb-border, radius: 5pt, inset: 9pt)[
      #text(size: 9.7pt)[The state's new water scheme was designed for twelve towns, but by the time the pipes reached the fourth the money had run out. The contractor blames the price of diesel; the ministry blames the contractor's accounting. Both agree that the survey, which took two years, was finished before the first pipe was laid.]
    ]

    #v(3pt)
    According to the passage, the survey was finished

    A. after the first pipe was laid \
    B. before the first pipe was laid \
    C. after the money ran out \
    D. at the same time as the fourth town was reached],
  [
    *Step 1 — name the type.* *According to the passage* means a stated fact: this is a detail question, so there is exactly one sentence and I can scan for it.

    *Step 2 — scan for the key word.* *Survey* appears once, in the last sentence: *the survey, which took two years, was finished before the first pipe was laid*.

    *Step 3 — read the sentence twice.* The relative clause *which took two years* is a passenger; the claim is *finished before the first pipe was laid*.

    *Step 4 — test the options.* A reverses the sentence. C is a true fact about the scheme but it is not about the survey. D invents a coincidence of timing the passage never mentions. B repeats the sentence exactly.

    *Check.* Put B back: “the survey was finished before the first pipe was laid” — the sentence now reads as the passage reads. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  2,
  "The word with two lives",
  [Read the passage, then answer the question.

    #v(3pt)
    #block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-light, stroke: 0.6pt + jamb-border, radius: 5pt, inset: 9pt)[
      #text(size: 9.7pt)[The board has declined to table the report until the auditor has seen it, and the chairman has said privately that he would rather the whole matter were postponed than examined in public.]
    ]

    #v(3pt)
    As used in the passage, *table* means

    A. to propose for discussion \
    B. to postpone \
    C. to examine \
    D. to publish],
  [
    *Step 1 — the two lives.* *Table* means *propose* in British usage and *postpone* in American usage. Both are correct English, so the word alone cannot decide it: the sentence must.

    *Step 2 — the syntax.* *Declined to table the report until the auditor has seen it* — the board has refused to do something with the report until it is checked.

    *Step 3 — the second clause.* The chairman *would rather the whole matter were postponed than examined in public*. The second sentence tells you what the first was for: the board wants delay, not discussion.

    *Step 4 — test.* *Declined to postpone* makes the two clauses agree. *Declined to propose for discussion until the auditor has seen it* would mean the board is eager to discuss it, which the second clause contradicts. C and D are senses the word does not have.

    *Check.* The sentence and the clause after it must agree; only B makes them agree. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  3,
  "The pronoun that stands for a clause",
  [Read the passage, then answer the question.

    #v(3pt)
    #block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-light, stroke: 0.6pt + jamb-border, radius: 5pt, inset: 9pt)[
      #text(size: 9.7pt)[The council raised the market levy without consulting a single trader, and this is what the association has objected to. Its spokesman said the traders would have accepted an increase that had been explained to them.]
    ]

    #v(3pt)
    What does *this* refer to in the first sentence?

    A. the market levy \
    B. the council \
    C. raising the levy without consulting the traders \
    D. the association's objection],
  [
    *Step 1 — name the type.* *What does this refer to* is a reference question, so the answer is in the sentence itself.

    *Step 2 — look at what comes before.* The clause before *this* is *the council raised the market levy without consulting a single trader* — an action, not a noun.

    *Step 3 — substitute.* “The council raised the levy without consulting a trader, and *the market levy* is what the association objected to” — the association did not object to a levy; it objected to how it was raised, as the second sentence confirms: the traders would have accepted an increase that had been explained.

    *Step 4 — the wrong options.* A is a noun from inside the clause rather than the clause itself. B is the actor, and the association has not objected to the council's existence. D reverses the sentence: the objection is what *this* caused, not what *this* is.

    *Check.* The test question is *what did the association object to?* — and the second sentence answers it in the traders' own terms. The answer is *C*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  4,
  "The question that is asked backwards",
  [Read the passage, then answer the question.

    #v(3pt)
    #block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-light, stroke: 0.6pt + jamb-border, radius: 5pt, inset: 9pt)[
      #text(size: 9.7pt)[The new rice mill employs ninety workers, most of them women from the surrounding villages. It buys paddy from four hundred farmers within a day's walk, and it has cut the cost of milling in the district by a third. The mill's own electricity comes from a diesel generator, and the generator has failed three times since the harvest began.]
    ]

    #v(3pt)
    Which of the following is NOT stated in the passage?

    A. The mill employs ninety workers. \
    B. The mill buys paddy from four hundred farmers. \
    C. The mill's generator has failed three times. \
    D. The mill sells rice more cheaply than the imported variety.],
  [
    *Step 1 — mark the negative.* The word is NOT, and it is the whole question. Three options are in the passage and one is not.

    *Step 2 — check each option against a sentence.* A: *employs ninety workers* — stated. B: *buys paddy from four hundred farmers* — stated. C: *failed three times since the harvest began* — stated. D: the passage says milling in the district costs a third less, which is about the cost of milling, not about the price against imported rice.

    *Step 3 — the near-miss.* D sounds like the passage because *cut the cost by a third* is there. But the passage compares the cost of milling before and after, not local rice with imported rice. That is the almost-true option: the right subject with a detail changed.

    *Check.* Three options can be pointed at in the text and one cannot, which is exactly what NOT asks for. The answer is *D*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  5,
  "Two plausible options, one sentence behind each",
  [Read the passage, then answer the question.

    #v(3pt)
    #block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-light, stroke: 0.6pt + jamb-border, radius: 5pt, inset: 9pt)[
      #text(size: 9.7pt)[For years the town's refuse was dumped at the edge of the stream, and the stream carried most of it away. When the dump was closed, the refuse did not disappear: it appeared in the streets, because the town had been given nowhere else to put it. The council's new bins have been used, but only by the streets that already had a collector. A bin is not a service; a collector is.]
    ]

    #v(3pt)
    Which of the following best expresses the writer's view?

    A. The town should reopen the dump by the stream. \
    B. The council's bins do not solve the problem because collection is what was missing. \
    C. The stream carried most of the refuse away. \
    D. The streets that had a collector are richer than the others.],
  [
    *Step 1 — name the type.* *The writer's view* is a main-idea question in the shape of an opinion, so the answer must be a claim covering the whole passage.

    *Step 2 — the verdict.* The last sentence is the writer's own: *a bin is not a service; a collector is*. Everything before it is evidence.

    *Step 3 — the two plausible options.* A and B both sound like a view. But A contradicts the passage, which shows that closing the dump was not the problem — the missing collection was. The passage never recommends reopening anything.

    *Step 4 — the two details.* C is the second sentence, lifted word for word: true, and a detail. D draws a comparison between rich and poor streets that the passage never makes.

    *Check.* The option that survives both tests — coverage of every sentence and a claim rather than a fact — is B. Put it at the top of the passage and the dump, the streets and the collectors all become evidence for it. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
)

#diagram-fig("diagrams/recap_ch02.svg",
  "Chapter 2 recap card: the question types.", width: 100%)

#block(width: 100%, fill: jamb-mint, stroke: 0.9pt + jamb-green, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  #text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 9.2pt, weight: "bold", fill: jamb-green)[Chapter 2 in one breath]
  #v(3pt)
  #text(size: 10.1pt)[
    Eight types, and the type tells you where the answer lives. Detail, vocabulary and reference are answered by finding a sentence: scan for the key word, read the clause twice, and substitute the option back into the sentence. Inference, tone and purpose are answered by taking one step from a sentence and never two. Negation — EXCEPT, NOT, FALSE, LEAST — gives three statements that are in the passage and one that is not, and it is answered by marking the negative word before reading a single option. Main idea is answered from every paragraph, and its wrong options are details. Three of the four options on any question are built from four templates: the true detail lifted from the passage, the almost-true with one word changed, the reasonable invention the passage never commits itself to, and the reversal that swaps cause for effect. Name the template before you name the answer.
  ]
]

== 2.5 Chapter 2 in the Exam Hall — a Two-Minute Set

#mcq(1, [“The board declined to table the report until the auditor had seen it.” As used here, *table* means], [propose for discussion], [postpone], [examine], [publish])

#mcq(2, [“The mill cut the cost of milling in the district by a third.” Which question does this sentence answer?], [Why did the mill fail?], [How much cheaper is milling now?], [Who owns the mill?], [When was the mill built?])

#mcq(3, [“The council raised the levy without consulting a trader, and this is what the association objected to.” The word *this* refers to], [the levy], [the council], [raising the levy without consultation], [the association])

#mcq(4, [“Which of the following is NOT stated in the passage?” A candidate who answers without marking the word NOT is most likely to], [run out of time], [choose a statement that is in the passage], [mistake the main idea for a detail], [misread a quotation])

#answer-key-grid(("B", "B", "C", "B"))

#answer-notes((
  ("B", [*declined to table until the auditor has seen it* means the board wanted the report delayed, and the clause that follows confirms it. A is the British sense of the word, and the passage deliberately rules it out; C and D are senses *table* does not have.]),
  ("B", [the sentence gives a figure — *a third* — so it answers a *how much* question. A is a question the passage never raises, and C and D are questions of ownership and date that the sentence does not touch.]),
  ("C", [*this* stands for the clause before it, which is an action rather than a noun. A is the noun inside the clause, B is the actor, and D is the response rather than the thing responded to.]),
  ("B", [a negation question sets three true statements against one absence, so a candidate who reads it as a positive question picks a true statement. A describes a time problem, C a different question type, and D a reading error rather than a polarity error.]),
))

